{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-269983",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If $f(1)=0$, then by the factor theorem $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ To find the other roots of the polynomial $f(x)$, we need to find the other factors of $f(x).$\n\nSince $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-10$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $-8$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-10x+8=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-8)=(x-1)(x-2)(x+4).\n$$\n\nNow that our polynomial is fully factored, we can see that the three roots of the polynomial, arranged in ascending order, are\n\n$$\nx=-4,\\,x=1,\\,x=2.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
